== Step 1: load test of one node (simulated, 120 s per row), SLO p99 <= 25 ms
   req/s    CPU %    p50 ms    p99 ms  meets SLO
     400      40%      2.96     18.78  yes
     450      45%      3.03     18.86  yes
     500      50%      3.15     19.12  yes
     550      55%      3.30     19.52  yes
     600      60%      3.49     20.04  yes
     650      65%      3.79     20.82  yes
     700      70%      4.14     22.04  yes
     750      75%      4.64     23.82  yes
     800      80%      5.39     27.07  NO
     850      85%      6.59     33.35  NO
     900      90%      9.01     45.86  NO
     950      95%     15.89     89.35  NO
CPU capacity 1000 req/s; safe rate (highest row meeting the SLO) 750 req/s = 75 % CPU

== Step 2: demand
daily average 1664 req/s; busiest hour 3150 req/s (peak-to-average 1.89)
busiest minute = busiest hour x 1.25 = 3938 req/s
growth 5 % per month for 9 months: x 1.551 -> planned peak 6108 req/s

== Step 3: nodes
ceil(6108 / 750) = 9 nodes carry the planned peak within the SLO
+1 spare so one node can fail or be updated at peak: 10 nodes
check: with one node down, each of 9 nodes takes 679 req/s (safe rate 750)

== Step 4: the naive plan, for comparison
average x growth / (CPU capacity x 70 %) = 2582 / 700 -> 4 nodes, no spare
at the planned peak those 4 nodes would each take 1527 req/s (CPU capacity 1000)
